        <div class="lnd-legal__body">

            <section id="scope">
                <h2>1. What this notice covers</h2>
                <p>
                    It applies to <strong>verichat.co.zw</strong> and the pages on it
                    (including this notice), the signed-in portal an approved account uses,
                    the API behind it, and the Verichat mobile application. It covers both
                    the public enquiry forms and everything we hold about people who already
                    have an account.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Verichat Quality Projects is the organisation that decides why this
                    information is kept, at 11 Lincoln Road, Avondale, Harare, Zimbabwe.
                    Where a client sits in South Africa we apply the same principles POPIA
                    would require there, and the contact in section 12 is the same person for
                    either.
                </p>
                <div class="lnd-legal__callout">
                    <p>
                        <strong>Status.</strong> This notice was written against the running
                        system and is published for the pilot. It has not yet been reviewed by a
                        legal practitioner; once it is, the version number and date at the top
                        of this page change.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="collect">
                <h2>2. What we hold about you</h2>
                <p>
                    Only what the work needs. There is no separate "usage profiling" store -
                    if a field exists, it exists because a screen in the portal reads it.
                </p>

                <div class="lnd-legal__group">
                    <h3>Your Verichat account</h3>
                    <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                        <li>Your name, your email address, the role you applied for and whether the account is switched on.</li>
                        <li>A salted hash of your password - never the password itself.</li>
                        <li>Whether an employee has approved the account, and who approved or declined it.</li>
                        <li>The signed-out time of a one-use token that proves you control that mailbox, and of a one-use token that lets a new password be set.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="lnd-legal__group">
                    <h3>The organisation you belong to</h3>
                    <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                        <li>Client name, sector (clinic, hospital, mine, educational institution), contract status and whether its equipment needs scheduled calibration.</li>
                        <li>A named contact person, their email and phone number, and a postal or street address.</li>
                        <li><strong>The latitude and longitude of the site.</strong> We keep coordinates so a delivery can be routed and a field engineer can plan a visit. For a sole-practitioner clinic that position describes a person, so we treat it as personal data and it is never published or shared outside the account team.</li>
                        <li>A diary of contacts with you, and the sales representative assigned to the account.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="lnd-legal__group">
                    <h3>Business you do with us</h3>
                    <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                        <li>Enquiries, the products asked for, quotations we send, orders and purchase orders.</li>
                        <li>Deliveries and their tracking numbers, dispatch and receipt times, and who signed for a consignment.</li>
                        <li>Service tickets - including any photographs you attach of a faulty unit - and the calibration history of equipment we installed.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="lnd-legal__group">
                    <h3>What you send us from a public page</h3>
                    <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                        <li>An enquiry or a request for access: your name, organisation, email, phone and the message you typed. It is stored as an inquiry so it can be answered, and is also emailed to our sales desk.</li>
                        <li>A sign-in help note: your name, email, the role you say you signed up as, your message, and whether it came from the website or the app. A second note from the same address within two hours is folded into the first rather than stored twice.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="lnd-legal__group">
                    <h3>The mobile app, and health-related screens</h3>
                    <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                        <li>A push token for the device, and the device name, type and last time it synchronised.</li>
                        <li>Where you use the teaching and telehealth screens: observations, prescriptions, laboratory results and appointment records you or your supervisor enter. That is special personal data. It is used only for the feature that opened it and is not joined to the sales record.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="lnd-legal__group">
                    <h3>Records of changes</h3>
                    <p class="lnd-legal__group-lead">
                        Every edit made inside the portal is written to an audit trail: who
                        changed it, which record, the value before and the value after, when,
                        and the IP address of the connection that made the change. We keep this
                        so that a figure on a delivery note or a calibration certificate can be
                        explained later. It is read by a System Administrator and is not shown
                        to client accounts.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="why">
                <h2>3. Why we hold it, and on what basis</h2>
                <table class="lnd-legal__table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">What</th>
                            <th scope="col">Why</th>
                            <th scope="col">Basis</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Identity and contacts</td>
                            <td>To supply, deliver and service the goods, and to answer you.</td>
                            <td>Performance of a contract</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Site coordinates</td>
                            <td>Route a delivery and book an engineer to the right place.</td>
                            <td>Performance of a contract</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Approval state, audit trail, IP address</td>
                            <td>Stop an unapproved account reading another organisation's prices, and make a disputed record explainable.</td>
                            <td>Legitimate interest - fraud and dispute control</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Calibration history</td>
                            <td>Equipment safety and the certificate that goes with it.</td>
                            <td>Legal and professional obligation</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Push notifications</td>
                            <td>Tell you a consignment arrived or a ticket moved, without you watching a screen.</td>
                            <td>Consent - switchable in the app's settings</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Email confirmation and password reset</td>
                            <td>Prove the person asking owns the address.</td>
                            <td>Legitimate interest - account security</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Health readings on teaching screens</td>
                            <td>Clinical training and the consultation you booked.</td>
                            <td>Explicit consent, and the care relationship behind it</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h3>Ranking an enquiry</h3>
                <p>
                    New enquiries are scored for urgency - sector, contract status, stated
                    need-by date and value - so that a scarce delivery slot goes to the site
                    that needs it first. The score is a queue position, not a judgement about a
                    person, and a sales representative reviews and can override every one of
                    them before anything is sent to you.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="who">
                <h2>4. Who inside Verichat can read it</h2>
                <p>
                    Access is per role, checked on the server for every request - not just
                    hidden in the menu you are shown.
                </p>
                <table class="lnd-legal__table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">Role</th>
                            <th scope="col">What they can see</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Client User</td>
                            <td>Only their own organisation: its enquiries, quotations, orders, deliveries, assets and tickets.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Medical Student</td>
                            <td>The catalogue, their own cart and quote requests, and the teaching records they create. No client file.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Sales Representative</td>
                            <td>The accounts and pipeline assigned to them, and the enquiry queue.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Procurement Manager</td>
                            <td>Suppliers, stock levels, purchase orders and warehouse alerts.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Logistics</td>
                            <td>Delivery addresses, coordinates and consignment status - enough to move a box.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Field Support Engineer</td>
                            <td>Installed assets, fault history and calibration dates for the sites they attend.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>System Administrator</td>
                            <td>Everything, including the audit trail and the user list. This role approves accounts.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>
                    Approving a self-registered account is limited to System Administrator,
                    Sales Representative and Procurement Manager. Nobody outside these roles,
                    and nobody at another company, is given access to your records. We do not
                    sell or licence them.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="accounts">
                <h2>5. Accounts, approval and the recovery links</h2>
                <p>
                    <strong>Signing up is an application, not access.</strong> The record is
                    written down and waits; a Verichat employee checks that the applicant is
                    genuinely a client and not somebody registering as staff, and only an
                    approved, confirmed account can sign in.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Confirmation and reset links.</strong> Both links carry a random
                    one-time code. We store only a SHA-256 hash of that code, never the code
                    itself. A reset link expires in one hour and stops working the moment it is
                    used; a confirmation link expires after seven days and is replaced when a
                    new one is requested. Nobody at Verichat can read a link you received.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>What a reset proves.</strong> Resetting a password proves you can
                    receive mail at that address. It does not turn a pending application into
                    an approved account, and it does not reopen an account that was declined or
                    switched off.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Forgot-password answers are deliberately neutral.</strong> The
                    message you get is the same sentence whether or not the address has an
                    account, so the form cannot be used to find out who is registered with us.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="devices">
                <h2>6. Cookies, device storage and what we do not run</h2>
                <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                    <li><strong>One essential cookie</strong> in the web portal. It is HTTP-only, holds a session reference rather than your credentials, and expires after 120 minutes without activity. It cannot be turned off while you are signed in; it sets nothing on your machine.</li>
                    <li><strong>No advertising, analytics or social cookies.</strong> No third-party tracker is loaded by the portal.</li>
                    <li><strong>Web fonts.</strong> The public pages ask Google for the Inter and Poppins stylesheets, so your IP address reaches Google for that request. The signed-in portal uses its own bundled files.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mobile sign-in token</strong> is kept in the phone's own secure store - the Keychain on iOS, the keystore-backed secure storage on Android - not in a file we control.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mobile offline cache.</strong> So the app still works in a site with no signal, recent responses and any actions you queued are held in the app's private storage, protected by the device's own encryption but not additionally encrypted by us. Clearing the app's data or signing out removes them. Keep the device locked.</li>
                    <li><strong>Push notifications</strong> need the device token, and switching notifications off in settings stops us sending them.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="others">
                <h2>7. Services we depend on</h2>
                <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                    <li><strong>Hosting.</strong> The portal and API run on our contracted application hosting provider. The database is on managed SQL Server with them. If your data is processed outside Zimbabwe, that is the reason - we will name the region for you on request.</li>
                    <li><strong>Email.</strong> An SMTP provider sends confirmation, approval, reset, help and delivery notices on our behalf. They move the message; they do not get to keep a business record of you, and the message body is not copied into the database - only the fact that a notice went out.</li>
                    <li><strong>Expo / app stores.</strong> The mobile build and its push channel pass through Expo, and the store you install from records your download. Both have their own privacy statements.</li>
                    <li><strong>Maps.</strong> Any embedded map on a contact page loads tiles from a third party, which sees your IP address while the map is displayed.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>We do not disclose your data to any of these parties for their own use.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="retention">
                <h2>8. How long we keep it</h2>
                <p>
                    For as long as the relationship lasts. A clinic that buys an autoclave from
                    us needs us to know its calibration history for the life of that machine,
                    and the audit trail behind a delivery note is kept because a disputed
                    invoice can arrive years later. Accounting and equipment-safety rules decide
                    those periods, not convenience.
                </p>
                <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                    <li>An application that was never approved does not have to sit in the queue forever - ask us and we clear it.</li>
                    <li>Sign-in help notes stay open until an administrator closes them; a closed note is kept with the name of whoever closed it.</li>
                    <li>Recovery codes expire on their own - one hour and seven days - and cannot be replayed after use.</li>
                    <li>Deactivating an account stops access immediately. The record itself is kept so the orders it placed still read correctly, which is why a deletion request is the one we take most care over.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    Ask for erasure at <a href="mailto:sales&#64;verichat.co.zw">sales&#64;verichat.co.zw</a>
                    and we remove what we can, then tell you in writing what we must keep and
                    under which obligation. There is no self-service delete button in the portal
                    today; a person handles the request.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="security">
                <h2>9. How we protect it</h2>
                <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                    <li>Connections to the deployed portal and API use HTTPS, with strict transport security in production.</li>
                    <li>Passwords: PBKDF2 with SHA-256 and a per-account salt. Recovery codes: random, hashed with SHA-256 before storage, single-use, dated.</li>
                    <li>Every read and write is authorised on the server against your role and, where records belong to a client, against your own account.</li>
                    <li>Forms carry an anti-forgery token, and free text is sanitised on the way in so that markup typed into a message box cannot become part of a page.</li>
                    <li>Database credentials live in the deployment environment, not in the source code.</li>
                    <li>Only the roles that need a permission have it; approval rights are limited to three roles.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    No system can promise that nothing will ever go wrong. If we become aware
                    of a breach that puts your information at risk, we will tell you and the
                    regulator without unreasonable delay, and say what happened and what we did
                    about it.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="rights">
                <h2>10. Your rights</h2>
                <p>
                    Write to the contact below from the email address on your account, and tell
                    us which of these you want. We answer within 30 days and we do not charge
                    for a first request.
                </p>
                <ul class="lnd-legal__list">
                    <li><strong>Know</strong> what we hold about you, and get a copy of it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Correct</strong> anything wrong - a contact person, a phone number, a site address.</li>
                    <li><strong>Have it deleted</strong>, subject to what we must keep by law.</li>
                    <li><strong>Object</strong> to a ranking or a use, and ask for a human to review it instead.</li>
                    <li><strong>Withdraw consent</strong> for notifications at any time in the app's settings, without losing the account.</li>
                    <li><strong>Complain</strong> to a supervisory authority: Zimbabwe's Data Protection Authority under the Cyber and Data Protection Act [Chapter 11:34], or - for a client in South Africa - the Information Regulator under POPIA.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    We do not knowingly collect information from children. Accounts are for
                    staff of a client organisation and for adults training in a health
                    programme.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="changes">
                <h2>11. Changes to this notice</h2>
                <p>
                    The date at the top of the page is the date this version was published, and
                    every version is numbered. If we change what we collect, who can read it or
                    what we do with it, approved accounts get an email before the change takes
                    effect and the portal shows the new notice at the next sign-in. Continuing
                    to use the service after that is your agreement to the revised notice.
                </p>
            </section>

